  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight... High pressure building over the eastern U.S. has \ncontinued a persistent pattern of gusty easterly winds and rounds of \nisolated to widely scattered onshore moving showers. Despite cloudy \nconditions, temperatures have climbed a few degrees above normal, \nmostly in the low to mid 80s, and added humidity has produced peak \nheat index values in the upper 80s and low 90s. Looking toward the \nevening, expect mild temperatures in the mid 70s alongside a few \nquick passing showers for any Halloween plans. Wind gusts up to 20 \nmph will be possible at times, particularly near the coast. \n\nBuilding seas and rough surf are expected at local beaches this \nafternoon and evening. A High Surf Advisory takes effect at 5PM \nas large breaking waves increase to 5-7 ft through the overnight \nperiod. A high risk of rip currents continues.\n\nSunday-Monday... Increasing moisture coupled with a passing \ndisturbance aloft will drive a wet, unsettled period. Continued, \nbreezy onshore flow should favor scattered onshore-moving showers \nearly Sunday. Coverage of scattered to numerous showers and an \noccasional storm builds through the afternoon and evening as pulses \nof shortwave energy begin to pass aloft. Moderate to high rain \nchances then continue through the night Sunday and into Monday. \nContinuing to monitor a heavy rainfall threat through this period as \nhigh-res ensemble means begin to show localized accumulations of 3-\n5 inches and 90th percentile values exceeding 5-7+ inches. A Slight \nRisk of Excessive rainfall exists along the coast each day, \nextending through portions of the interior, including the Orlando \nMetro on Monday. \n\nIn addition to wet conditions, a number of coastal hazards will \nremain present at area beaches. A high risk of life-threatening rip \ncurrents continues alongside large breaking waves and rough surf. \nEntering the water is not advised! While coastal flooding concerns \ncontinue to slowly ease, model guidance at Lake Worth Pier continue \nto run near Moderate Flood Stage during the afternoon high tide \ncycle, and a Coastal Flood Advisory will continue through at least \nSunday afternoon. \n\nCloudy conditions and increased rain chances will keep afternoon \ntemperatures slightly below seasonal values, ranging the upper 70s \nand low 80s. Sunday morning temperatures remain mild in the low to \nmid 70s, warmest along the coast. A few degrees cooler Monday \nmorning, mostly in the low 70s.\n\nTuesday-Friday... A cold front tracks across the central U.S. into \nmid week, finally pushing a persistent area of high pressure \noffshore the eastern U.S. How the synoptic pattern then evolves \nbecomes more uncertain as major deterministic models begin to \ndiverge. Onshore flow should steadily weaken through midweek, but \ndeep moisture should generally persist through at least Tuesday, \ncontinuing moderate rain chances. Reductions in rain chances are \nforecast into the following days as a drier air mass advects across \nthe local area. High temperatures mostly in the mid 80s fall to \nmostly range the low 80s by the end of the work week.",
